## Corrected physical quaternion retest

Canonical project mapping retained by the tested wrapper:

ROS x = SDK quaternion[1]
ROS y = SDK quaternion[2]
ROS z = SDK quaternion[3]
ROS w = SDK quaternion[0]

The wrapper normalizes this reordered quaternion once and supplies the same result to `sensor_msgs/msg/Imu.orientation` and the dynamic `unilidar_imu_initial -> unilidar_imu` TF.

Corrected Position A, robot/sensor stationary:

x = 0.009059164131498416
y = -0.031178789865725306
z = -0.12216226094401264
w = 0.9919789295185631

Corresponding TF roll/pitch/yaw:

roll = approximately +1.47 degrees
pitch = approximately -3.41 degrees
yaw = approximately -14.09 degrees

Corrected Position B, after approximately 90 degrees clockwise on the floor when viewed from above:

x = -0.0036949123342495515
y = -0.03330593011191569
z = -0.7866761503106011
w = 0.6164557544339373

Corresponding TF roll/pitch/yaw:

roll = approximately +2.76 degrees
pitch = approximately -2.69 degrees
yaw = approximately -103.90 degrees

Computed relative rotation, Position B times inverse(Position A):

angle = approximately 89.755 degrees
axis x = approximately 0.01589
axis y = approximately -0.00884
axis z = approximately -0.99983

Conclusion: PASS. The corrected physical binary reports the clockwise planar rotation almost entirely around robot/LiDAR -Z, consistent with the documented upright mounting. This closes the component-order defect recorded by IMU-ORIENT-001-R01. The test verifies the project mapping and tested planar direction; it does not establish a globally referenced heading, arbitrary 3D orientation accuracy, or the physical correctness of `initial_imu_frame -> imu_frame` for every downstream interpretation.

